  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Implementation\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding a target value within a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which would take O(n) time), binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half:\n\n1. Compare the target to the middle element.\n2. If they match, you're done.\n3. If the target is smaller, discard the right half.\n4. If the target is larger, discard the left half.\n5. Repeat until the target is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThis gives us **O(log n)** time complexity \u2014 dramatically faster than linear search for large datasets.\n\n---\n\n## The `lower_bound` Function\n\nThe `lower_bound` function finds the **first index** where the target could be inserted while maintaining sorted order. In other words, it returns the index of the **first element that is greater than or equal to** the target. If no such element exists (the target is larger than all elements), it returns the length of the list.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar('T')\n\n\ndef lower_bound(sorted_list: List[T",
